course: dictionary
lesson: 6737
title: Olympiad
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An olympiad is a contest where pupils solve hard problems, for example in maths, physics or chess. The word can also mean a set of four years between Olympic Games, or the Olympic Games themselves. Look at the words around it to find out which meaning fits.
explain: «In an ethics olympiad, school teams are given short stories called cases, each with a dilemma, where every choice has something costly about it.» Here the words teams, cases and choice show that an olympiad is a contest. A team has to work together and solve a problem, not just run a race.
explain: «In history, the ancient Greeks counted time in olympiads of four years.» This sentence is about time, not a school contest. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Put in the meaning you have chosen and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: An olympiad is a contest where pupils solve hard problems.
fact: Maths, science and chess can all have olympiads.
fact: An Olympiad can also mean a four-year gap between Olympic Games.
fact: «Olympiad» is a noun, and it can be made plural: olympiads.
fact: «In an olympiad puzzle about soft words you are a detective: first turn every soft phrase into plain words.» This shows that olympiad puzzles need careful, step-by-step thinking.
exercise: «In an ethics olympiad, school teams are given short stories called cases.» What is an olympiad? | *A contest where pupils solve hard problems | A type of school dinner | A map of the school grounds | A kind of sports shoe
exercise: «In history, the ancient Greeks counted time in olympiads of four years.» How long was one olympiad in this sentence? | Ten years | Four days | Four weeks | *Four years
exercise: «This olympiad lesson mixes the shop lessons of the course - prices, change, offers and unit prices - into puzzles.» Find and copy one word that means the money given back to you when you pay. | *change | mixes | course | puzzles
test: «The last olympiad puzzles of the course don't ask "true or false?" but the harder question: how could you find out, and which result would settle it?» What does the harder question ask you to do? | Copy the answer from a friend | *Think how you could find out, and which result would settle it | Just say yes or no | Guess quickly and move on
test: Which subjects can an olympiad be in? | Only spelling | Only cooking | Only football | *Maths, physics or chess
exam: Tom says an olympiad is a meal you eat at lunchtime. Which meaning is correct? | *A contest where pupils solve hard problems | A meal eaten at lunchtime | A map of the playground | A type of school bag
